    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/untrusted-input.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "What counts as attacker-controlled",
      "prov:value": "**The derived case is the one that hides.** Measured here 2026-09-18: this\nrepo's staging workflow bound `head.ref` to `env` in one step, then wrote the\n**raw** branch to `$GITHUB_OUTPUT` and interpolated `steps.slug.outputs.branch`\ntwo steps later. The sanitised `slug` output beside it was safe; the `branch`\noutput was the original value under a new name. **Sanitise at the boundary or\ncarry it in `env` the whole way — never both, half each.**",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/untrusted-input.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "What counts as attacker-controlled",
      "prov:value": "**And this paragraph went unchecked for four days while a gate was written\nagainst the opposite premise.** `check-workflow-injection.ts`, 2026-09-22,\nclassified `steps.*.outputs` as safe *unconditionally*; a dispatch input reached\na `mv` through a step output and the gate reported nothing, found 2026-09-26\n(bean `6bhf`). The gate now resolves the producing step, and it grades a\nsanitised output free text anyway — **narrower than the \"Trusted\" row above, on\npurpose**: a person may judge a `sed` sufficient, a gate cannot, and the remedy\nis one `env:` line either way. See\n[`injection-boundaries`](../security/injection-boundaries.md)\n§\"`steps.*.outputs` is not a class\".",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 6bhf",
        "2026-09-22",
        "2026-09-26"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-22",
        "2026-09-26"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/untrusted-input.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`pull_request_target` is where this stops being theoretical",
      "prov:value": "On `pull_request`, a fork PR gets a read-only token and no secrets, so an\ninjection runs on the runner and little else. **`pull_request_target` runs with\nthe base repository's token and secrets.** This repo's staging-cleanup job ran\non `pull_request_target: [closed]` with `contents: write` and interpolated a\nfork branch name — so closing a PR from a branch with a crafted name was\narbitrary code execution with write access. Fixed the same day it was looked\nfor; it had been there for as long as the workflow had."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/untrusted-input.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A workflow GitHub cannot parse does not fail loudly",
      "prov:value": "**A run whose name is a FILE PATH rather than the workflow's `name:` is a parse\nfailure.** GitHub had no `name:` to read. `AGENTS.md` records two workflows that\nsat red for a day in 2026-08 this way; adding the `env:` bindings above\nreproduced it, by putting a second `env:` on a step that already had one."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/untrusted-input.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A workflow GitHub cannot parse does not fail loudly",
      "prov:value": "Two hand-rolled attempts at that duplicate check reported false findings before\nthe real parser went in — `types:` under two different triggers, then `run:` in\ntwo different steps. Both would have produced a wall of noise in a repository\nwith no duplicates at all, which is exactly how a check gets switched off. YAML\nscoping is a parser's job."
    }
  ]
}
